---
// firma2-project-map — where the project actually is. A grayscale basemap
// carrying this project's work areas as drawn shapes, plus the same work areas
// written out as a text list directly beneath. The client asked for this
// section specifically: a restoration project is a place before it is a budget
// or a schedule, and every other section of this page is a table of numbers.
//
// LAST IN THE MAIN COLUMN, AND AS BIG AS IF IT LED. Those are two decisions and
// this section has now had every combination of them. It closes the page because
// the page owes one question an answer above the fold — "is this project doing
// what it said it would" — and WHERE is not that question; leading, this section
// put zero pixels of tracked data on the first screen. It keeps the full width
// and the full height because room and position are different currencies, and
// only the second one was the problem. So: the last thing you reach, and the
// whole screen when you reach it.
//
// NO CARD, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS VARIANT. Every other section on the
// page is a card because every other section is a block of TEXT that needs an
// edge to be a block. A map is not a block — it is a surface, and it is the only
// section here that gets better the more of it there is. So this one leaves the
// card behind and takes the whole column: full-bleed to the pane's edges, a
// screenful tall. The chrome that a card used to carry — its title, its Add
// affordance, its legend — comes back as panels floating ON the map, which is
// the convention every map UI already uses and is what the card was standing in
// for.
//
// TWO VARIANTS, AND ONLY THE SIZING DIFFERS. `section` is everything described
// above — the last section of a scrolling column, which has to work out its own
// width and height because nothing around it knows it wants a screenful.
// `canvas` is the same surface handed a box that is already the right size, and
// it simply fills it; firma2-project-atlas is the caller, where this map is not
// a section of the page but the page itself, with the record floating on it.
// The canvas, the plates, the shapes, the tooltips and the whole client module
// are shared verbatim — a second map component would have been 250 lines of
// duplicated Leaflet for three sizing declarations, which is how two maps in one
// product start rendering the same project differently.
//
// FULL-BLEED MEANS ITS COLUMN, NOT THE WINDOW. On the project detail page the
// column is everything left of the record rail, and the two meet edge to edge:
// this canvas bleeds a step further right than its track so the rail's own
// surface covers the overrun and there is no seam of page background between
// them. Nothing floats on this surface — there was a version that let the page
// slot the record's cards over it, and the note in fitBounds records why a rail
// beside the map is the better trade.
//
// TWO SHAPES, BECAUSE RESTORATION WORK HAS TWO SHAPES. WorkArea.kind === 'area'
// is a polygon (a treatment unit, a marsh basin — `path` is a ring and Leaflet
// closes it); 'reach' is a polyline (a planting corridor, a fuel-break
// alignment — `path` is a route and closing it would invent a boundary the data
// does not claim). They are styled to stay distinguishable at a glance and in
// grayscale print: a translucent fill under a thin stroke versus a heavy
// unfilled line.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: ecology has NO map lego. Checked the full
// node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components catalog — the only `esa-map` in the
// hub is src/pages/design-system/components/esa-map.astro, a `type="reference"`
// documentation page explicitly marked non-functional, with no .astro or .ts
// implementation to import. Same situation as esa-grid vs. AG Grid, which this
// spoke already resolved the same way in firma2-project-grid.astro. Beacon is
// not cloned on this machine, so tier 2 is unavailable. Leaflet 1.9.4 from
// cdnjs is the vetted precedent in the sibling spoke cb-fish-design
// (src/pages/map-sow.astro, src/components/map-sow/cbf-msow-map.astro), and
// this file is the reusable home for the pattern in this spoke.
//   Everything AROUND the map is still a lego: esa-button (chrome) is the Add
//   affordance, esa-empty-state the no-boundaries case, and the legend is
//   `.grid` + `.cluster` + typography composites rather than hand-rolled
//   flexbox. The floating panels are the one thing this variant adds and they
//   are NOT a reinvented card: esa-card was tried and is wrong here — it draws a
//   header band, a body padding step and a title row sized for a section of a
//   document, where what a map needs is a small opaque plate that sits ON the
//   canvas. Checked the catalog for a floating-surface lego (esa-popover and
//   esa-tooltip are both anchored transients that open and close; there is no
//   persistent panel). Beacon is not cloned on this machine. The plate is three
//   declarations off the floating-surface tokens, the same ones esa-popover
//   itself resolves.
//   The other bespoke visual is the shape tooltip:
//   esa-tooltip is a Lit component that wraps a trigger in the light DOM, and
//   the thing being annotated here is an SVG path inside Leaflet's overlay pane
//   that Leaflet re-projects on every pan and zoom — it has no stable box to
//   anchor to, so Leaflet's own tooltip (which the projection already moves) is
//   the only correct owner. It is themed from tokens below, not restyled by eye.
//
// COLOUR COMES FROM THE DATA-VIZ RAMP, READ AT RUNTIME. Leaflet takes concrete
// colour strings — it paints SVG attributes, not CSS — so the two shape colours
// are resolved out of the live custom properties with getComputedStyle rather
// than hardcoded. That keeps one source of truth: re-point the categorical
// tokens in theme-firma2.css and the map follows. Slots 1 and 2 specifically,
// because that ramp's ORDER is searched for colour-vision separability, so the
// first two slots are the safest pair in the system to put side by side.
// Grayscale CARTO Positron underneath: design-principles reserves colour for
// content, and on this screen the work areas ARE the content.
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import type { ProjectDetail, WorkArea } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  /** The project whose work areas are drawn. Only mapCenter, mapZoom and
   *  workAreas are read; the whole record is taken so the page passes one
   *  prop and this section can grow into the rest of it without a signature
   *  change. */
  detail: ProjectDetail;
  /**
   * WHICH BOX THIS SURFACE IS SIZED AGAINST — and it is only the sizing that
   * differs; the canvas, the plates, the shapes and the script are one
   * implementation.
   *
   * `section` (default) is a section IN a scrolling column: it gives the
   * content pane's inline padding back with a negative margin and computes its
   * own height off the fold, because nothing around it knows it wants to be a
   * screenful. That is the project detail page's last section.
   *
   * `canvas` is the whole pane: it fills whatever positioned box it is dropped
   * in (`block-size: 100%`, no bleed, no floor) and lets that parent own the
   * arithmetic. That is firma2-project-atlas, where the map IS the page and the
   * record floats on it — the parent already has to size itself against the
   * fold to place its own panels, so a second copy of that calculation in here
   * would be a second answer that can drift from the first.
   */
  variant?: 'section' | 'canvas';
}

const { detail, variant = 'section' } = Astro.props;
const { workAreas, mapCenter, mapZoom } = detail;

// "4.1 miles", "62 acres". Formatted ONCE, server-side, and shipped in the
// payload — the legend row and the shape's tooltip are the same fact in two
// places, and two format sites is how they drift apart.
const extentLabel = (area: WorkArea) => `${area.extent} ${area.extentUnit}`;

// Everything the client script needs, serialized rather than fetched. `path`
// travels verbatim; Leaflet reads [lat, lng] pairs, which is exactly what
// WorkArea.path already is.
const mapConfig = {
  center: mapCenter,
  zoom: mapZoom,
  areas: workAreas.map((area) => ({
    label: area.label,
    kind: area.kind,
    path: area.path,
    extentLabel: extentLabel(area),
  })),
};
---
<!-- An UNNAMED <section>: with no accessible name it is a plain grouping
     element, not a landmark, so this adds a styling and scripting root without
     stacking a second region inside whatever landmark the page puts it in. The
     <h3> in the head plate below is the section's heading — it was the card's
     before the card went, at the same typography role the card gave it, so the
     document outline is unchanged by the reskin. -->
<section class="firma2-project-map" data-variant={variant}>
  <!-- CANVAS FIRST AND ABSOLUTE. Everything after it in this file is a plate
       laid ON it, which is the whole difference from the carded version: there,
       the map was a box in a column and the legend came after it; here the map
       IS the column and the legend has to sit somewhere over it. Source order
       still reads map → title → legend → facts, which is the order a screen
       reader wants; only the painting is stacked. -->
  <!-- role="application" follows the cb-fish precedent, and it is accurate
       here rather than decorative: Leaflet's keyboard handler puts
       tabindex="0" on this container and binds the arrow keys to pan and
       +/- to zoom, which is custom key handling a virtual cursor would
       otherwise swallow. It is not a trap — Tab still leaves, and Leaflet
       1.9.4 ships no `outline: none`, so the container and the zoom buttons
       keep the token focus ring from focus.css untouched.
       A canvas of tiles is opaque to assistive tech no matter what role it
       wears, which is why the legend plate is real content and not an
       afterthought. -->
  <div
    class="firma2-project-map__canvas"
    role="application"
    aria-label="Work areas map"
  ></div>

  <!-- THE HEAD PLATE IS THE CARD HEADER, UNBUNDLED. Same two things the card
       put in its header row — the section's name and the one action a
       collection takes — now on a plate at the surface's leading corner, which
       is where a map UI has put its title and its tools since before the web.

       ADD, not edit. This section is a COLLECTION, and that is what separates
       it from Key facts: those hold scalar values, so each one got a per-field
       affordance that appears on hover and edits in place, and their section
       pencils went away. A set has no single field to hover — you add to it,
       remove from it, reorder it — so the section keeps one control and it
       names the act it actually performs.

       Still inert: nothing is wired behind it yet. It is a real, enabled
       <button> so the eventual handler has somewhere to land, and deliberately
       not `disabled` — disabled claims the action is unavailable to THIS user,
       which is a different and false statement. Per-row editing and removal
       inside this section are not built yet. -->
  <div class="firma2-project-map__plate firma2-project-map__plate--head cluster" data-gap="md">
    <h3 class="firma2-project-map__title typography-title-sm-strong">Work areas</h3>
    <EsaButton variant="chrome" size="sm" icon="plus">Add area</EsaButton>
  </div>

  <!-- THE LEGEND AND THE TEXT ALTERNATIVE ARE THE SAME ELEMENT. A visually
       hidden equivalent would be a concession nobody sees; on screen this
       also does the job every map legend does — says what the green and the
       blue mean — and adds the extents, which the shapes cannot show at all.
       A <ul> because it is a countable set of peers, and the count is the
       first thing a screen-reader user needs.

       IT IS A PLATE AT THE FOOT NOW, not a row under the map, and it keeps the
       LEADING corner rather than the trailing one — the trailing side is where
       the facts panel floats, and a legend tucked under it would be the one
       piece of chrome the reader has to hunt for.

       The empty branch is unreachable with the current portfolio — every
       project in firma2-project-detail.ts authors at least one area — but
       the prop is public, and an empty <ul> on a live map is a worse
       answer than saying so. -->
  <div class="firma2-project-map__plate firma2-project-map__plate--legend">
    {workAreas.length > 0 ? (
      <ul class="firma2-project-map__legend stack" data-gap="2xs">
        {workAreas.map((area) => (
          <li class="cluster" data-gap="sm">
            <!-- The swatch mirrors the shape it stands for — a filled square
                 for a polygon, a bar for a line — so the mapping is legible
                 without relying on hue alone. -->
            <span
              class={`firma2-project-map__swatch firma2-project-map__swatch--${area.kind}`}
              aria-hidden="true"
            ></span>
            <span class="typography-body-md">
              <!-- The swatch carries area-vs-reach visually and is hidden from
                   AT; this is that same distinction in words, and "reach" is
                   the term the data model and the domain both use. -->
              <span class="visually-hidden">{area.kind === 'area' ? 'Area. ' : 'Reach. '}</span>{area.label}
            </span>
            <span class="firma2-project-map__extent typography-body-md">{extentLabel(area)}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    ) : (
      <EsaEmptyState
        size="sm"
        title="No work areas mapped"
        description="Add a work area to map this project."
      />
    )}
  </div>

  <!-- Shape data for the client module. A <script type="application/json"> is
       inert markup, not executable, and it is how the sibling grid hands rows
       to its own island. -->
  <script type="application/json" data-map-config set:html={JSON.stringify(mapConfig)}></script>
</section>

<script>
  type LatLng = [number, number];

  interface MapArea {
    label: string;
    kind: 'area' | 'reach';
    path: LatLng[];
    extentLabel: string;
  }

  interface MapConfig {
    center: LatLng;
    zoom: number;
    areas: MapArea[];
  }

  /** The payload of `firma2-map:reflow` — a composing surface's own reservation,
   *  in Leaflet's two-corner form (`[x, y]` pixels). Both halves are optional;
   *  omitting one falls back to the plate reservation this section draws for
   *  itself. See the listener near the foot of this script. */
  interface ReflowDetail {
    paddingTopLeft?: [number, number];
    paddingBottomRight?: [number, number];
  }

  // Leaflet is a CDN global, not an npm dependency of this spoke (same version
  // and same CDN as cb-fish-design), so there are no types to import and `any`
  // is the honest annotation rather than a fictional interface.
  type Leaflet = any;

  const LEAFLET_BASE = 'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/leaflet/1.9.4';

  let leafletReady: Promise<Leaflet> | null = null;

  /**
   * Load Leaflet and resolve when `window.L` exists.
   *
   * THIS IS THE ONE REAL HAZARD IN THIS FILE. Astro hoists a processed <script>
   * into the <head> as type="module", so this code can run BEFORE a plain
   * <script src> written further down the body — document order is not
   * execution order, and reading window.L at module scope would find undefined.
   * So the component injects its own <link> and <script> and awaits the load
   * event instead of trusting placement. Self-contained and order-independent:
   * this section works wherever a page drops it, and works if a page drops two
   * of it (the data- attributes make the injection idempotent, and a tag that
   * is already in flight is simply awaited rather than duplicated).
   */
  function loadLeaflet(): Promise<Leaflet> {
    const w = window as unknown as { L?: Leaflet };
    if (w.L) return Promise.resolve(w.L);
    if (leafletReady) return leafletReady;

    if (!document.querySelector('link[data-leaflet-css]')) {
      const link = document.createElement('link');
      link.rel = 'stylesheet';
      link.href = `${LEAFLET_BASE}/leaflet.min.css`;
      link.setAttribute('data-leaflet-css', '');
      document.head.append(link);
    }

    let tag = document.querySelector<HTMLScriptElement>('script[data-leaflet-js]');
    if (!tag) {
      tag = document.createElement('script');
      tag.src = `${LEAFLET_BASE}/leaflet.min.js`;
      tag.async = true;
      tag.setAttribute('data-leaflet-js', '');
      document.head.append(tag);
    }

    const script = tag;
    leafletReady = new Promise<Leaflet>((resolve, reject) => {
      // Re-checked inside the executor: the tag may have finished loading
      // between the check above and this line, and a load event already fired
      // never fires again.
      if (w.L) {
        resolve(w.L);
        return;
      }
      script.addEventListener(
        'load',
        () => (w.L ? resolve(w.L) : reject(new Error('Leaflet loaded without window.L'))),
        { once: true },
      );
      script.addEventListener('error', () => reject(new Error('Leaflet failed to load')), { once: true });
    });
    return leafletReady;
  }

  /** Resolve a design token to the concrete string Leaflet needs for an SVG
   *  attribute. Read off <html>, which is where BaseLayout sets data-theme, so
   *  this picks up the spoke's theme and not just the hub defaults. */
  const token = (name: string, fallback: string) =>
    getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(name).trim() || fallback;

  async function initProjectMap(root: HTMLElement) {
    const canvas = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-map__canvas');
    const configEl = root.querySelector<HTMLScriptElement>('[data-map-config]');
    if (!canvas || !configEl?.textContent) return;

    const config: MapConfig = JSON.parse(configEl.textContent);

    const L: Leaflet | null = await loadLeaflet().catch(() => null);
    if (!L) {
      // The CDN is down or blocked. Drop the box rather than leave a grey
      // rectangle that looks like a map that failed to draw — the legend below
      // is the same information in text and is already on screen, so nothing
      // needs to be said about it in copy.
      canvas.hidden = true;
      return;
    }

    // scrollWheelZoom off: a map inside a scrolling page that eats the wheel
    // traps the reader halfway down the record. Drag, double-click, the zoom
    // buttons and the keyboard all still zoom.
    const map = L.map(canvas, { scrollWheelZoom: false }).setView(config.center, config.zoom);

    // THE BASEMAP IS THE ONE THING ON THIS SECTION THAT TOKENS CANNOT REACH.
    // Every other Leaflet surface here — the canvas behind the tiles, the zoom
    // bar, the attribution strip, the shape tooltips — is painted from semantic
    // roles in the <style> block below, so all of it followed the dark scheme
    // the day the scheme existed. The tiles are raster PNGs rendered on CARTO's
    // servers, so the only lever is WHICH RASTER SET to ask for, and it has to
    // be chosen in script. Leaving it on `light_all` put a white map, at full
    // brightness, in the middle of an otherwise dark page — the single loudest
    // thing on the screen, and on the atlas variant it is the whole screen.
    //
    // `dark_all` is the same cartography as `light_all` from the same source at
    // the same zooms, inverted — not a different map. So the grayscale basemap
    // this section was designed around (roads and streams legible, nothing
    // competing with the work areas drawn on top) holds in both schemes.
    const basemapUrl = () =>
      document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-scheme') === 'dark'
        ? 'https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png'
        : 'https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}{r}.png';

    const tiles = L.tileLayer(basemapUrl(), {
      attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors &copy; CARTO',
      subdomains: 'abcd',
      maxZoom: 19,
    }).addTo(map);

    // THE SHAPE STYLES ARE FUNCTIONS, NOT CONSTANTS, and that is the whole of
    // why: `token()` reads a computed custom property, which resolves against
    // whatever `data-scheme` says AT THE MOMENT IT IS CALLED. Held in a const at
    // init, the categorical ramp is frozen at the scheme the page loaded in —
    // theme-firma2.css re-points categorical-1 from #3e9b4f to #46a758 in dark,
    // so a reader who switched schemes with the map on screen kept the light
    // green over dark tiles. Rebuilt per call, the same function serves the
    // first paint and every repaint after it.
    //
    // A unit has an inside, so it gets one: a light wash that lets the basemap's
    // roads and streams read through, under a thin edge that is the actual claim
    // being made about the boundary.
    const areaStyle = () => {
      const areaColor = token('--color-background-dataviz-categorical-1', '#3e9b4f');
      return {
        color: areaColor,
        weight: 2,
        opacity: 0.9,
        fillColor: areaColor,
        fillOpacity: 0.22,
      };
    };

    // A reach has no inside — it is a line of work along a channel — so the
    // weight goes into the stroke instead of a fill. Rounded joins because these
    // follow a watercourse, and mitred corners on a meander read as a survey
    // line rather than a stream.
    const reachStyle = () => ({
      color: token('--color-background-dataviz-categorical-2', '#3e63dd'),
      weight: 4,
      opacity: 0.95,
      lineCap: 'round',
      lineJoin: 'round',
    });

    const shapes = config.areas.map((area) => {
      const shape =
        area.kind === 'area'
          ? L.polygon(area.path, areaStyle())
          : L.polyline(area.path, reachStyle());

      // Sticky so the label tracks the cursor across a large polygon instead of
      // pinning to a centroid that may be off screen at this zoom.
      shape.bindTooltip(`${area.label} — ${area.extentLabel}`, {
        sticky: true,
        direction: 'top',
        className: 'firma2-project-map__tip',
      });

      return shape.addTo(map);
    });

    // FIT TO THE SHAPES, not to mapCenter/mapZoom. The authored center is a real
    // watershed coordinate but the zoom is one hand-picked number per project,
    // and a project whose second work area is nudged a couple of kilometres off
    // center can hang half a unit past the frame at that zoom. The bounds are
    // computed from the geometry actually on the map, so the frame is right by
    // construction. setView above still runs first, which is what makes the
    // authored center/zoom the fallback for a project with no shapes at all —
    // and gives Leaflet the view it requires before any layer is added.
    // FRAME AROUND WHATEVER IS ON TOP OF THE SURFACE, and WHAT is on top is not
    // always this component's business. The two defaults below are the plates
    // this file draws itself — the head at the leading top corner, the legend at
    // the leading foot. A composing surface that floats MORE over the canvas
    // (firma2-project-atlas floats the whole record on it) knows its own panels'
    // widths and nothing else does, so it passes its own reservation in through
    // the reflow event rather than this file guessing at a layout it cannot see.
    const fitToShapes = (
      paddingTopLeft: [number, number] = [32, 96],
      paddingBottomRight: [number, number] = [32, 132],
    ): void => {
      if (shapes.length === 0) return;
      map.fitBounds(L.featureGroup(shapes).getBounds(), { paddingTopLeft, paddingBottomRight });
    };

    // THE ONE THING A PARENT CAN ASK THIS SECTION TO DO. Leaflet caches its
    // container's size and paints tiles for that cache, so a canvas whose box
    // changed — the atlas hiding the page header band, which gives the map
    // another 100-odd pixels — renders a strip of grey until it is told. There
    // is no resize event for "my parent restyled me", so the parent says so.
    //
    // An EVENT rather than an exported function, because the two are in separate
    // Astro islands with no module boundary between them: the atlas holds a DOM
    // node, not an instance. It is also the honest direction of the dependency —
    // this section publishes a capability and does not know who calls it.
    root.addEventListener('firma2-map:reflow', (event: Event) => {
      const detail = (event as CustomEvent<ReflowDetail>).detail;
      map.invalidateSize();
      fitToShapes(detail?.paddingTopLeft, detail?.paddingBottomRight);
    });

    // THE COLUMN CAN CHANGE WIDTH WITHOUT THE WINDOW CHANGING SIZE, and Leaflet
    // has no way to know. It listens to `resize` on the window and nothing else,
    // so every layout move that is not a window move leaves it painting tiles for
    // a box that is no longer there — a grey strip down whichever edge grew. Both
    // of this page's rails can now be dragged and collapsed (bcn-edge-handle), so
    // this is no longer an edge case: it is one drag away on every visit.
    //
    // A ResizeObserver rather than a listener for the handle's event, because the
    // dependency runs the wrong way otherwise — the map would have to know which
    // controls exist on the page it happens to be laid out in. Watching its own
    // box catches every cause, including the nav rail collapsing, which was
    // already silently wrong before any of this.
    //
    // invalidateSize ONLY — deliberately not fitToShapes. Re-framing on a resize
    // would throw away a pan or a zoom the reader had just made, which is the
    // more annoying failure of the two. `firma2-map:reflow` stays the way a
    // parent asks for the frame back.
    //
    // rAF-coalesced: a drag fires the observer on every frame it moves, and
    // invalidateSize forces a layout read plus a tile pass.
    let reflowFrame = 0;
    new ResizeObserver(() => {
      if (reflowFrame) return;
      reflowFrame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        reflowFrame = 0;
        map.invalidateSize();
      });
    }).observe(canvas);

    // FOLLOW THE SCHEME WHILE THE PAGE IS OPEN. Reading data-scheme once at init
    // covers a reader who arrives already dark; it does not cover the two cases
    // that actually happen — someone changing the setting on the Settings screen
    // and coming back, and a "System" reader whose machine flips at sunset with
    // this page still on screen (src/lib/scheme.ts rewrites the attribute live
    // for exactly that). Both surface here as one attribute mutation.
    //
    // A MutationObserver on <html>, which is the same mechanism esa-chart uses
    // to re-theme itself (it watches data-theme / data-scheme / a11y-assurance).
    // There is no event for "a custom property resolved to a different value",
    // so the attribute that caused it is the signal.
    //
    // setUrl swaps the raster set in place: Leaflet keeps the tiles already
    // painted until the replacements load, so the map darkens rather than
    // blanking. setStyle re-reads the ramp for shapes that are already drawn —
    // `config.areas` and `shapes` are built by one .map() call, so index i is
    // the same work area in both, which is what makes the kind lookup honest.
    new MutationObserver(() => {
      tiles.setUrl(basemapUrl());
      config.areas.forEach((area, i) => {
        shapes[i].setStyle(area.kind === 'area' ? areaStyle() : reachStyle());
      });
    }).observe(document.documentElement, { attributeFilter: ['data-scheme'] });

    if (shapes.length > 0) {
      // FRAME AROUND THE PLATES, NOT INTO THE RAW BOX. A symmetric `padding` was
      // right when this was a card with nothing on top of it. Full-bleed, both
      // leading corners carry a plate — the title and Add at the top, the legend
      // at the foot — so a symmetric fit can put a work area behind an opaque
      // panel, the one failure mode a map-as-backdrop has that a map-in-a-card
      // does not. Leaflet's asymmetric form takes the two corners separately, so
      // the block axis reserves the plates' height and the inline axis stays
      // even: the shapes centre horizontally in a frame that clears them.
      //
      // The trailing edge needs nothing. It did while the record floated over
      // this surface — the reservation was measured off that panel — and it does
      // not now that the record has a rail of its own beside the map. Whatever
      // is to the right of this box is no longer this component's problem.
      fitToShapes();
    }

    // AND THE OTHER HALF OF THE HANDSHAKE. The fit above used this section's own
    // reservation, because at this point it is the only one it has — and on the
    // project detail page that is the whole story, since nothing else is on the
    // canvas. A composing surface that floats its own panels here cannot correct
    // that on load without knowing WHEN there is a map to correct: this module
    // waits on a CDN script, so it finishes an unknowable number of frames after
    // the page's other islands have run and there is nothing to poll for.
    // So it says so. The atlas listens for this and answers with the reflow it
    // would otherwise only have sent on the first click.
    root.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('firma2-map:ready'));
  }

  /** Idempotent start — the guard is on the DOM so the load-time sweep and the
   *  late-arrival seam below can never double-init one root. */
  const startProjectMap = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    if (root.dataset.f2MapInit !== undefined) return;
    root.dataset.f2MapInit = '';
    void initProjectMap(root);
  };

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-map').forEach(startProjectMap);

  // MAPS THAT ARRIVE AFTER LOAD. The project peek panel clones a record — map
  // included — out of a <template>, whose content the sweep above can never
  // see. The cloner announces the new subtree with this event (bubbling, from
  // the subtree's root), the same seam shape firma2-editable-field uses for
  // its own cloned rows. A root that is ALREADY running answers with a reflow
  // instead: a cached clone re-enters the DOM with Leaflet's size cache stale,
  // and invalidate + refit is what a re-shown map needs, not a second map.
  document.addEventListener('firma2:map-init', (event) => {
    const target = event.target;
    if (!(target instanceof HTMLElement)) return;
    const roots = target.matches('.firma2-project-map')
      ? [target]
      : Array.from(target.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-map'));
    roots.forEach((root) => {
      if (root.dataset.f2MapInit !== undefined) {
        root.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('firma2-map:reflow', { detail: {} }));
      } else {
        startProjectMap(root);
      }
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  /* ---- THE SURFACE ---------------------------------------------------------
     FULL-BLEED, AND THE BLEED IS A NEGATIVE MARGIN BECAUSE THE PANE'S PADDING
     IS NOT THIS COMPONENT'S TO REMOVE. esa-app-shell pads its content region a
     uniform --spacing-500, which every other section on the page wants; this is
     the one that does not, so it gives that step back on the inline axis and
     leaves the pane's rule alone. --spacing-500 is read from the same token the
     shell sets it with, so the two cannot drift.

     HEIGHT IS THE FOLD MINUS WHAT IS ABOVE IT. 100dvh is the target — dvh, not
     vh, so mobile browser chrome cannot clip it — less the page header band and
     the one stack step above this section.

     THE BAND IS SUBTRACTED FOR THE ATLAS. In firma2-project-atlas the band and
     this surface share one unscrolling frame, so the fold really is 100dvh less
     the band. On the record page the band scrolls away before this section
     fills the frame (it stopped pinning 2026-09-11), so the same subtraction
     leaves the map a band short of the fold there. One rule for both is kept
     on purpose: a map ~109px under the fold is a map, and a second sizing
     rule per mode is the kind of fork that drifts.

     THE BAND'S HEIGHT IS READ, NOT GUESSED. This started as a single measured
     constant and that was wrong for a reason worth keeping written down: the
     band is not one height. It is taller with a breadcrumb than without, and
     taller again the moment a long project name wraps at a narrow width — so a
     constant is correct at exactly one viewport and leaves a strip of page
     showing, or pushes the map's bottom edge past the fold, at every other.
     firma2-page-header measures itself and publishes that number on :root, so
     this subtracts the live value and
     re-solves on resize. The fallback is the measured height of the two-line
     case, which is the worst one to be wrong about.

     The remaining term is the stack step above this section (`data-gap="xl"` on
     the page's own stack) — the gap between it and Milestones, and the same
     value the band would have contributed when this led the column.

     The min-block-size is the floor that keeps this a map and not a letterbox
     on a short window. */
  .firma2-project-map {
    --firma2-map-offset: calc(var(--firma2-band-height, 148px) + var(--spacing-600, 2rem));

    /* WHERE THE PLATES SIT, AS A KNOB RATHER THAN A CONSTANT. The step itself
       is unchanged — --spacing-400 from whichever edge each plate takes — but
       the leading inset is now a name a composing surface can re-point, because
       the leading edge is the one that gets covered. firma2-project-atlas floats
       the record panel there and pushes the plates clear of it with one
       declaration; when that panel is hidden the same declaration goes away and
       they return to the canvas edge.
       Only the leading inline inset is exposed. The block insets never move (the
       plates take the top and the foot of the surface however wide the panels
       are) and the trailing edge carries no plate — Leaflet's attribution has
       the trailing foot and this file does not place anything against it.

       THE KNOB IS NOT DECLARED HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. A custom
       property declared on this element would SHADOW the one an ancestor set,
       so the composing surface's value would never arrive — the plates would sit
       at the canvas edge under whatever was covering them, which is exactly the
       bug this knob exists to prevent. The default lives in the two rules that
       read it, as var()'s fallback, which is the form that leaves the name free
       to inherit. */
    --firma2-map-plate-inset: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);

    position: relative;
    margin-inline: calc(var(--spacing-500, 1.5rem) * -1);
    block-size: calc(100dvh - var(--firma2-map-offset));
    min-block-size: 30rem;
    /* Leaflet stacks its own panes up to z-index 700 (popupPane), which is high
       enough to punch through an overlay later. A new stacking context keeps all
       of it local to this section, so the plates below can be ordered against
       the canvas with small numbers and nothing here can rise over the app's
       rail or a dialog. */
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* THE CANVAS VARIANT GIVES ALL THREE SIZING DECLARATIONS BACK. Every one of
     them above exists because a section in a scrolling column has to work out
     for itself how big it wants to be; dropped into a box that is already the
     right size, each of them is wrong. The bleed would push a full-pane map a
     step past the pane it was handed, the fold arithmetic would subtract a
     header band its parent has already subtracted (and, in the atlas, one that
     can be hidden outright), and the 30rem floor would overflow a short window
     rather than letterbox it — the parent is the box the reader can see, so a
     minimum here can only escape it.
     `block-size: 100%` needs a parent with a definite height and a positioned
     one to lay the plates against; firma2-project-atlas is both, and states so
     where it sets them. */
  .firma2-project-map[data-variant='canvas'] {
    margin-inline: 0;
    block-size: 100%;
    min-block-size: 0;
  }

  /* NO HEIGHT OF ITS OWN ANY MORE. This used to be a 640px box inside a card,
     with the legend as a row beneath it; both of those are gone, and the canvas
     is now simply everything the section is — inset: 0 rather than a stated
     size, so the fold arithmetic lives in exactly one place above.
     No border and no radius either: an edge is what a box in a column needs to
     separate itself from the column. Bled to the pane's edges there is nothing
     to separate from, and a hairline tracing the viewport would read as a
     rendering artefact rather than as a frame. */
  .firma2-project-map__canvas {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    /* Leaflet paints #ddd behind the tiles until they arrive. One neutral step
       off the page instead, so the first paint is a surface rather than a hole. */
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken, #f0f0f0);
    /* Leaflet hardcodes 12px Helvetica on .leaflet-container and everything
       inside inherits it — the attribution and the shape tooltips both. Naming
       the body-sm ROLE here re-homes them on the type scale in one declaration.
       The role tokens rather than the composite class because the class cannot
       be put on elements Leaflet creates at runtime. */
    font-family: var(--typography-body-sm-font-family);
    font-size: var(--typography-body-sm-font-size);
    line-height: var(--typography-body-sm-line-height);
    /* The section above already opened the stacking context; this only claims
       the floor of it, so every plate below paints over the tiles by source
       order without needing a number of its own. */
    z-index: 0;
  }

  /* ---- THE PLATES ----------------------------------------------------------
     THE SHARED PLATE, and it is one rule rather than three because the only
     thing that differs between the head, the legend and the facts panel is
     WHICH CORNER they take. Everything that makes them read as floating — the
     surface, the hairline, the radius, the shadow — is the same thing said once.

     `elevation-floating` and `elevation-2` are the tokens the system already
     uses for a surface that sits over content rather than in it; the shape
     tooltip further down this file resolves the same pair, which is what keeps
     a plate and a tooltip on this map looking like the same product.

     Absolutely positioned against the section, and the inset step is
     --spacing-400 on both axes so all three plates share one margin from the
     surface's edges. */
  .firma2-project-map__plate {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    padding: var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem) var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-floating, #fcfcfc);
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default-subtle, #d9d9d9);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md, 0.5rem);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-2, 0 2px 12px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04));
  }

  /* Leading top corner: the section's name and its one action. `.cluster` is
     already holding the two side by side; this only places the plate. */
  .firma2-project-map__plate--head {
    inset-block-start: var(--firma2-map-plate-inset);
    inset-inline-start: var(--firma2-map-plate-inset-inline-start, var(--firma2-map-plate-inset));
  }

  /* The title carries the card's own typography role, so it is the same heading
     it was — the margin reset is only because it is an <h3> outside the card
     that used to zero it. */
  .firma2-project-map__title {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Leading bottom corner. It sits above Leaflet's attribution strip, which
     takes the TRAILING bottom corner — the two never meet.
     A max-inline-size because a legend is a list of names and an unbounded
     plate would stretch to the longest one; 22rem is the same measure the
     facts panel opposite it takes, so the two plates are the same column width
     on either side of the map. */
  .firma2-project-map__plate--legend {
    inset-block-end: var(--firma2-map-plate-inset);
    inset-inline-start: var(--firma2-map-plate-inset-inline-start, var(--firma2-map-plate-inset));
    max-inline-size: 22rem;
  }

  /* THE PLATES TRAVEL WHEN THE LEADING INSET IS RE-POINTED, and the motion is
     the whole of what tells a reader the two are related — the record panel
     leaves and the map's own chrome moves into the room it vacated.

     --transition-base IS THE ROLE FOR EXACTLY THIS, and its own token doc names
     the case: "a change big enough to want to be watched — a panel resizing, a
     floating button arriving." Naming the role rather than a duration also gets
     the reduced-motion answer for free: @esa/tokens re-points the tier-2
     transition tokens to --duration-0 inside its own
     `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` block, so a hand-written media
     query here would be a second copy of a rule the system already applies. */
  .firma2-project-map__plate {
    transition: inset-inline-start var(--transition-base, 200ms ease);
  }

  /* `.stack` is the layout engine now, not `.grid`. The grid's 18rem floor was
     for a full-width card, where two work areas sat side by side; on a plate
     over the map the legend is a narrow column and one area per row is the only
     arrangement that fits. This only strips the list chrome that came with
     choosing <ul> for its semantics. */
  .firma2-project-map__legend {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* NO OVERLAY RULE ANY MORE, and the deletion is the point. This section
     briefly carried an `overlay` slot on its trailing edge that the project page
     floated the record's cards into, plus a matching reservation in fitBounds so
     the shapes framed clear of them. Both are gone: the record has a real rail
     beside the map now (firma2-record-rail), so nothing covers this surface and
     neither component has to know where the other's content is allowed to be. */

  /* The extent is the qualifying measurement, not the name — one step back in
     the text ramp, same size, so the labels stay the scanning line. */
  .firma2-project-map__extent {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
  }

  /* Sized in `em` rather than px so the swatch tracks whatever the row's type
     composite resolves to — the type scale clamps with the viewport, and a
     pinned px swatch drifts out of proportion at the ends of that clamp. */
  .firma2-project-map__swatch {
    flex: none;
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs, 0.125rem);
  }

  /* The same wash-under-a-stroke the polygon gets on the map. color-mix is what
     applies the fill opacity to a token: the categorical ramp is opaque by
     design (it has to carry a chart series), and copying its hex just to add an
     alpha channel would fork the value. */
  .firma2-project-map__swatch--area {
    inline-size: 0.875em;
    block-size: 0.875em;
    background: color-mix(
      in srgb,
      var(--color-background-dataviz-categorical-1, #3e9b4f) 22%,
      transparent
    );
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid
      var(--color-background-dataviz-categorical-1, #3e9b4f);
  }

  /* A bar, not a square — the polyline's shape, at the polyline's weight. */
  .firma2-project-map__swatch--reach {
    inline-size: 1.125em;
    block-size: 0.25em;
    background: var(--color-background-dataviz-categorical-2, #3e63dd);
  }

  /* ---- Leaflet's own chrome, re-homed on the tokens -------------------------
     :global() because Leaflet builds these nodes at runtime, so they never
     receive Astro's scope attribute. Each selector is still anchored to
     .firma2-project-map, which DOES carry it, so nothing here escapes this
     section. Values only — no layout, no focus suppression: Leaflet 1.9.4's
     stylesheet contains no `outline: none`, and none is added here, so the
     container and the zoom buttons keep the ring focus.css gives them. */

  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-control-attribution) {
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-raised, #fcfcfc);
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
  }

  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-control-attribution a) {
    color: var(--color-content-link, #2a7e3b);
  }

  /* THE HEAD PLATE TOOK LEAFLET'S CORNER. Leaflet puts its zoom control at
     top-left by default and this variant now floats a title plate in exactly
     that spot, so the +/− buttons rendered UNDERNEATH it — present, focusable,
     and invisible. The control corner is pushed down past the plate rather than
     the control being moved to another corner, because every other corner is
     taken too: the record panel has top-right, the legend bottom-left and
     Leaflet's own attribution bottom-right.

     A margin on the corner container, not a position on the control: Leaflet
     appends every top-left control to this same element, so anything added
     later (a scale bar, a layer switcher) clears the plate for free. */
  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-top.leaflet-left) {
    margin-block-start: 5rem;
  }

  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-bar) {
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default, #cecece);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm, 0.25rem);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-1, 0 1px 4px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06));
  }

  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-bar a) {
    background-color: var(--color-background-elevation-raised, #fcfcfc);
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-border-default-subtle, #d9d9d9);
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  .firma2-project-map :global(.leaflet-bar a:hover) {
    background-color: var(--color-background-default-hover, #f0f0f0);
  }

  /* The shape tooltip. Leaflet's default is a white box with a 0.4-alpha black
     shadow; this is the same box built from the floating-surface tokens so it
     reads as this product's popover rather than as the library's. */
  .firma2-project-map :global(.firma2-project-map__tip) {
    padding: var(--spacing-200, 0.5rem) var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-floating, #fcfcfc);
    border: var(--border-width-default, 1px) solid var(--color-border-default, #cecece);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm, 0.25rem);
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-2, 0 2px 12px 0 rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.04));
  }

  /* Leaflet draws the tooltip's arrow as a transparent border box and colours
     the one edge facing the shape. Only the top edge is styled because the
     tooltip is bound `direction: 'top'` and Leaflet honours that even while
     sticky, so the other three classes never land on this element. */
  .firma2-project-map :global(.firma2-project-map__tip.leaflet-tooltip-top::before) {
    border-top-color: var(--color-border-default, #cecece);
  }
</style>
